      "id": "section-10-4",
      "title": "Scope of Quantum Advantage",
      "number": "10.4"
    }
  ],
  "opening": "In Chapters 7 and 9, we saw quantum algorithms that solve problems using fewer oracle queries than any classical procedure. When does a reduction in queries justify claiming that quantum computers are more powerful than classical ones?",
  "opening_continuation": "We first fix how computational resources and problem difficulty are measured and compared. We then prove that the \\(\\sqrt{N}\\) query count for unstructured search cannot be improved, and examine the scope and limits of quantum advantage.",
  "intro_continuation": "For example, in Chapter 9 we saw a quantum search algorithm that finds a marked item among \\(N\\) candidates using about \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\sqrt{N}\\) queries. Does that mean no quantum algorithm could ever find the marked item in far fewer queries—say, \\(\\log_2 N\\)—or is \\(\\sqrt{N}\\) a fundamental limit? Answering such questions requires a precise way to measure costs and prove limits on all possible algorithms."
}
